{"parentUuid":"1b12907d-d3bf-4f21-a208-fe08ac290713","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01AzMTxpGkKJ6Tbq6Lu2AyQ2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question — and worth pushing on. Let me lay out what's actually different between agents, then what could be streamlined.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- Each agent transcript has genuinely different JSON shapes — this isn't convention drift, it's that Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, etc. are different products. But the *structure* of the walker (open file, scan JSONL, handle missing, yield ToolCalls) is identical across them.\n- When you see \"six files that look similar,\" the question to ask is: *what's actually varying?* If the answer is \"four lines per file,\" that's a sign the abstraction boundary is in the wrong place.\n- There's a second, more radical question hiding here: do we even need to walk transcripts locally, given the server already derives tool-time from them?\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n## What's actually per-agent vs. boilerplate\n\nHere's what the two walkers I looked at are doing:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────┐\n│ Step                                    │ Per-agent?       │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤\n│ Open file, handle missing/empty path    │ No (identical)   │\n│ bufio.Scanner w/ 16MiB buffer           │ No (identical)   │\n│ For each line: unmarshal JSON           │ No (identical)   │\n│ Decide \"is this a tool call?\"           │ YES              │\n│ Extract tool name                       │ YES              │\n│ Extract timestamp                       │ YES (diff paths) │\n│ Extract bash command from args          │ YES              │\n│ Collect, return                         │ No (identical)   │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────┘\n```\n\nAbout 60% of each walker file is boilerplate. The per-agent part is \"given one parsed line, did I find a tool call, and if so what's in it.\"\n\n## Why formats really are different (not just stylistic)\n\n- **Claude Code**: `type==\"tool_use\"` blocks nested inside `message.content[]`, timestamp at line level\n- **Codex**: four distinct payload types (`function_call`, `local_shell_call`, `custom_tool_call`, `web_search_call`), each with different argument shapes\n- **Gemini**: `toolCalls[]` array on messages, different struct entirely\n- **OpenCode**: doesn't have a native JSONL format in this repo at all — has to shell out to `opencode export` to get one\n- **Copilot**: timestamp on every line, `toolCallId` convention\n\nSo \"one walker for all\" isn't realistic — the formats are actually different. But \"one walker + a tiny per-agent extractor\" is realistic.\n\n## The streamlined version\n\nA single helper in the `agent` package:\n\n```go\n// package agent\nfunc WalkJSONLTranscript(path string, extract func([]byte) ([]ToolCall, error)) ([]ToolCall, error)\n```\n\nEach agent implements just:\n\n```go\n// package claudecode\nfunc (c *ClaudeCodeAgent) WalkToolCalls(path string) ([]agent.ToolCall, error) {\n    return agent.WalkJSONLTranscript(path, extractClaudeCodeToolCalls)\n}\n\nfunc extractClaudeCodeToolCalls(line []byte) ([]agent.ToolCall, error) {\n    // 20-30 lines of per-agent extraction, no file-handling concerns\n}\n```\n\nThat kills the boilerplate, keeps the per-agent logic honest, and makes adding Gemini basically a 30-line extractor function. It's the right shape.\n\n## The bigger question: do we need local walking at all?\n\nWorth raising because I think it's actually the load-bearing architectural question, not \"how many walkers\":\n\nThe server *already* does tool-time derivation on committed checkpoints. That's part of the canonical-label pipeline. It's the long-term source of truth for both Labels and ToolProfile.\n\nThe only reason local walking exists is to fill in **active (un-committed) sessions** — the ones showing up in the \"today\" tab that the server hasn't seen yet.\n\nThree real paths:\n\n**A. Keep local walking, streamline it.** Extract `WalkJSONLTranscript` helper, reduce each walker to ~30 lines. Add Gemini, defer the rest. This is the answer I'd default to.\n\n**B. Drop local walking entirely.** Active sessions show \"awaiting analysis\" in the Agents tab; committed sessions show full tool-time from the server. Simpler code, slightly worse live-UX. The whole `ToolCallWalker` interface goes away.\n\n**C. Keep what's there and live with it.** Two walkers forever, document the limitation, move on.\n\n**My honest view:** A is best. The walker *capability* is worth having — live agent tool-time on the \"today\" tab is a differentiator for a feature called `entire activity`. But shrinking each walker to a 30-line function is strictly better than the current structure, *and* it makes the \"should we add Gemini\" question trivial because the marginal cost falls to near-zero.\n\nWant me to do that refactor — extract the helper, shrink Claude Code + Codex walkers — before we decide about Gemini?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2558,"cache_read_input_tokens":94037,"output_tokens":4371,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2558},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":4371,"cache_read_input_tokens":94037,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2558,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2558,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaHYCoQX4MHiwDY3QeTve","type":"assistant","uuid":"cf911540-c821-4e82-b430-49925c6e9559","timestamp":"2026-04-21T20:22:57.923Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entir