{"parentUuid":"71a56e0d-33ff-4ec5-82af-8d0b7afa1aaa","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01V5xXyk446mr8a5LTJ8jrEn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Right — \"team\" oversells it when there's no team object. Options, from most natural to most accurate:\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The data on the right column is really \"activity on this repo from everyone who touches it.\" Whatever we call it, we should match what the label *means*, not just what sounds good.\n- Labels shape expectations. \"Team\" implies there's a definable team (\"who's on my team?\"). \"Everyone\" just says *everyone using this repo* — no structure implied, true to the data.\n- Naming the column after the repo (e.g., `entireio/cli`) collapses the ambiguity: the reader immediately knows the scope.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n## Options\n\n| Label | Feels | Tradeoff |\n|---|---|---|\n| **everyone** | natural, inclusive | slight ambiguity (everyone in what?) — resolved by context |\n| **contributors** | accurate, matches API | slightly technical |\n| **repo** | scope-explicit | reads as \"the repo itself\" rather than \"other people\" |\n| **`<org/repo>`** | concrete | long, changes when you switch repos |\n| **others** | short, direct | excludes you by definition — useful if header is \"me vs others\" |\n\n**My lean: `everyone` as the column label, with the repo name in the panel title for scope clarity.** So you'd see:\n\n```\n╭─ Agents · entireio/cli · Week ─────────────────────╮\n│                       me       everyone             │\n│   claude-code         12          89                │\n│   Sessions                                          │\n│   ...                                               │\n```\n\nAlternative if you want more precision: **`contributors`**. Matches the server-side language (`contributor-tokens`, `contributor-agents`), so a reader who peeks at the