{"parentUuid":"60b029c1-4d83-433a-adbe-dd7537fa7ec3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016o3NUCtdAnbxxVuLkFss7d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Got it — this clarifies the whole auth pattern.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- **PR #1578** made `/api/v1/stats/checkpoints` and `/api/v1/stats/commits` bearer-auth (CLI-accessible). Existing `/api/v1/cache/user/*` stayed session-auth (browser-only).\n- **PR #1590** added `/api/v1/stats/activity` — a single server-aggregated endpoint returning dashboard-ready summary + daily/hourly agent breakdown + per-repo rollups. The pattern: \"don't build stats in the CLI, let the backend do it and expose a CLI-accessible aggregate.\"\n- **PR #1593** renamed `/api/v1/stats/*` → `/api/v1/me/*`.\n- **Our recap code is hitting `/api/v1/cache/:org/:repo/overview/*`** — those are `optionalAuth` + `rateLimitUnauthenticated`. On paper bearer should work, but `checkRepoAccess` does a `user_repos` join on whatever userId the auth middleware extracts. If the bearer-token-derived userId isn't joining, we get \"repo not found\" / empty data — exactly what you've been seeing.\n- **The established pattern** (per PRs #1578/1590/1593): make a dedicated bearer-auth `/api/v1/me/*` endpoint that returns **pre-aggregated per-agent data** (tokens, sessions, checkpoints, labels, skills, MCP, tool-mix). CLI consumes one endpoint; server does all the grouping.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n## Proposed server endpoint\n\n`GET /api/v1/me/recap?timeframe=last-week&repo=entireio/cli` (bearer auth, mirrors `/me/activity` + `/me/commits` style).\n\n**Response shape** — per-agent aggregates including the \"contributors\" comparison:\n\n```ts\n{\n  timeframe: \"last-week\",\n  repo?: string,  // optional: scope to one repo\n  me: {\n    tokens: number\n    sessions: number\n    checkpoints: number\n    labels: { label: string; count: number }[]\n    skills: string[]\n    mcpServers: { name: string; count: number }[]\n    toolMix: { shell: number; fileOps: number; search: number; mcp: number; agent: number; other: number }\n    models: string[]\n    repos: { repo: string; sessions: number }[]\n  }\n  byAgent: {\n    [agentName: string]: {\n      me: <same shape as above, scoped to this agent>\n      contributors: <same shape, aggregated across all repo contributors for this agent>\n    }\n  }\n  contributors: {\n    distinctUsers: number\n    totalTokens: number\n    totalSessions: number\n    totalCheckpoints: number\n  }\n  updatedAt: string\n}\n```\n\nServer-side implementation reuses:\n- `handleUserCheckpoints` — what user's checkpoints in the range\n- `getByCheckpointIds` (batch analyses) — labels/skills/mcp/toolProfile per cp\n- `repo-overview.*` queries — contributor aggregates\n\nThe aggregation is exactly what my CLI-side code is doing now, just moved to the server.\n\n## What this replaces in the CLI\n\nOnce this endpoint exists, our `recap/contributors.go` + `enrich.go` collapse into a single `GET /api/v1/me/recap` call. No batch fetching, no stampRepos, no per-cp analysis round-trips.\n\n## Scope question\n\nDo you want me to:\n\n**A.** Draft the server-side PR on entire.io (add `/api/v1/me/recap`)?\n\n**B.** Stop iterating on CLI-side workarounds and just wait for the endpoint to exist?\n\n**C.** Keep CLI-side fixes to make what's there work in the meantime, knowing the right solution is the server endpoint?\n\nMy lean: **A** — the CLI churn has made the gap obvious, and the server PR is straightforward given the existing aggregation code. Once it lands, the CLI side is a clean one-endpoint consumer.\n\nWhich do you want?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3402,"cache_read_input_tokens":876514,"output_tokens":4088,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","c